# Cloudflare Workers static-assets response headers (wrangler/client.jsonc).
#
# index.html and version.json must never be served from a stale edge/browser cache, or the
# auto-reload in src/platform/web/autoReload.ts would keep reading the pre-deploy hash and a
# reload would just re-fetch the old page. Everything under /assets/ carries a Rollup content
# hash in its filename, so those are safe to cache forever.
/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache, must-revalidate
/version.json
  Cache-Control: no-cache, must-revalidate
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# ## The art, and why it needed a rule of its own (2026-09-21)
#
# Everything this file did not name fell to the platform default, which is
# `public, max-age=0, must-revalidate` — measured on the live deploy, on `/ui/hub_bg.png`,
# `/skins/orb-core/frames.json` and `/audio/ui-tap_00.mp3` alike. `max-age=0` means the
# browser may keep the bytes but must ask before using them, so a RETURNING player re-opened
# a conditional request for every one of the ~200 shipped art and audio files. They come back
# 304 with no body, which is why this was invisible in a bytes-transferred view — but a round
# trip per file, batched behind the connection limit, is the same wait whether or not bytes
# come back with it, and it sits in front of the menu (the `lobby` pack) and in front of the
# first run (everything else).
#
# These files are NOT content-hashed — `/ui/hub_bg.png` keeps its name across deploys — so
# `immutable` is not available here the way it is for `/assets/*`: it would pin a player to
# whatever art they first downloaded, with no URL change to ever release them. An hour of
# freshness plus a week of `stale-while-revalidate` is the trade instead: a returning player
# inside the hour makes no request at all, one after it paints instantly from cache while the
# revalidation runs in the background, and new art reaches them on the visit after that.
#
# The one thing that makes this safe to trade away is a rule from design/12: art is pure
# presentation and never feeds the engine. A player briefly running a deploy old in its
# textures and new in its code sees the older icon; they cannot desync, because no byte of
# this influences the simulation. Code has the opposite property and gets the opposite rule —
# hashed filenames and `immutable` above.
/ui/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, stale-while-revalidate=604800
/skins/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, stale-while-revalidate=604800
/weapons/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, stale-while-revalidate=604800
/biome/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, stale-while-revalidate=604800
/environment/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, stale-while-revalidate=604800
/audio/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, stale-while-revalidate=604800
